# 3. REAL ELEVATION, SLOPE, ROUGHNESS AND TRAVERSABILITY

Build a terrain analysis pipeline using actual georeferenced elevation data when available.

Calculate:
- Elevation and relative elevation.
- Local slope.
- Terrain roughness.
- Surface discontinuities where supported by the data.
- Potentially difficult terrain regions.
- Data coverage and confidence or uncertainty.

Use suitable numerical methods and preserve the source resolution. Document the algorithms and units.

Generate terrain cost layers that can be used by the route planner.

Important scientific limitations:
- A rover photograph alone cannot reliably determine absolute elevation, physical distance, slope, depth, or real-world object dimensions without suitable calibration or spatial data.
- Image brightness and color must not be treated as direct elevation measurements.
- Do not infer precise slope or depth from a single uncalibrated image.
- When only image-space analysis is possible, label results as image-space estimates or visual heuristics.
- Never display invented elevation values or false spatial accuracy.

